Nepal is highly susceptible to natural disasters due to its mountainous terrain and major rivers.

Last month, Nepal and Tibet suffered deadly flash floods triggered by an avalanche that killed about 1450 people and left more than 4500 missing.

The latest flooding is a result of a late monsoon triggered by a low-pressure system that originated over the Bay of Bengal, according to Nepal's Meteorological Department.

The precipitation has since eased, although light rain is expected in parts of the country next week.

In the Kanchanpur district of western Nepal, a major suspension bridge collapsed due to flooding on the Mahakali river on Wednesday, forcing authorities to close the structure.

